-- title:
--   Formal smoothness of the descended supersingular chart B₀
-- statement:
--   The setting is the following. Let $q$ be a prime with $q = 2$, let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and let $\ell_g$ be a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $q$ in the sense of `LiesOverPrime`, i.e. $q \in A^{\mathrm{nonunits}}$ (`hA`). Let $W$ be a finite set of places of the field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A, M')$ over the residue field of $A$, assumed (`hW`) to consist exactly of the supersingular places `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, that is the rational places $w$ lying in the affine geometric locus whose value $w.\mathrm{evalAt}(j^{\mathrm{geom}})$ lies in the supersingular $j$-set `ssJSet q`; let $s \in W$ be one of them. Let `hle` assert $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M' \le \mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, the latter being the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the function field of $X_{H}(q^2M')$ for $H =$ `levelH q M'`, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$. Let $R_0$ be a constant reduction of $A$ on $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ with values in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A, M')$ (a valuation subring of integers, a surjective residue map with kernel the maximal ideal, compatible with $A$ and its residue map, together with the place map and its degree and divisor compatibilities), and `hR₀` requires that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, that element lies in the integers of $R_0$ and its $R_0$-residue, viewed as a Laurent series over the residue field of $A$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   On the arithmetic side, $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$, $\pi_0 \in k_0$ lies in $A$ (`hπ`), and $\mathcal O := A \cap k_0$ denotes the comap of $A$ along $k_0 \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$. The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` state that $\mathcal O$ is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, is Henselian, and has algebraically closed residue field; `hκ` states that every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0$ lying in $A$. Further data: a prime $\ell \ge 3$ with $\ell \ne q$ and $\ell \nmid M'$, an element $\zeta_0 \in k_0$ which is a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, an element $\varpi_t \in k_0$ lying in $A$ with $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$, an intermediate field $K_b$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/k_0$ assumed trivial ($K_b = \bot$) with a valuation subring $A_b$ induced from $A$ (`hAb`) and a generator $\varpi_b \ne 0$ of its maximal ideal, a tameness hypothesis `htame` that $(\ell_g-1)/2$ is a unit of $\mathcal O$, and an element $\xi \in k_0$ which is a primitive $q$-th root of unity. The level subgroup is $H_1 \le (\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ with $H_1 =$ `levelH q M'` $\cap \ker\big((\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times\big)$ (`hH₁`).
--
--   The geometric data are: an intermediate field $K_\ell$ of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(k_0)$ over $k_0$, carrying an $\mathcal O$-algebra structure compatible with $k_0$, with $K_\ell = \mathrm{laurentBaseChange}\,k_0\,(\mathrm{xHFunctionField}\,(q^2M')\,H_1)$ (`hKℓ`); an element $j_\ell \in K_\ell$, nonzero, equal to the coefficientwise image of the $j$-series $\mathrm{jq}$ (`hjℓ`); an element $\varpi_t' \in \mathcal O$ mapping to $\varpi_t$ (`hϖt'`); a maximal ideal $y$ of the finite chart algebra $C := \mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,\mathcal O\,K_\ell\,j_\ell$ (the elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $\mathcal O[j_\ell]$) containing the image of $\pi_0$ (`hy`, `hϖy`); an $\mathcal O$-subalgebra $B_t$ of $K_\ell$ and a valuation subring $W_t$ of $K_\ell$ with $B_t \subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`); natural numbers $n, m$ with $1 \le n$, $n \mid q+1$, $1 \le m$ and $\pi_0^m = \varpi_t' w$ for a unit $w$ of $\mathcal O$ (`hn1`, `hnq`, `hm1`, `hm`); an element $\gamma_0 \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ together with a $k_0$-automorphism $\tau_0$ of $K_\ell$ which is a level automorphism at $\gamma_0^{-1}$ in the sense of `IsLevelAutAt` (the $q$-expansion identity characterising the action of $\gamma_0^{-1}$ on quotients of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$, normalised by $\xi$) and preserves $W_t$ (`hτ₀W`); an element $\zeta_c$ of the adic completion of $\mathcal O$ with $\zeta_c^n = 1$ and $\zeta_c^k - 1$ a unit for $0 < k < n$; a finite set `ends` of subrings of $K_\ell$; an element $a_0 \in \mathcal O$ such that the $q$-expansion generator $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,q$ lies in $K_\ell$ (`hjK`) and in $C$ (`hjC`), with $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,q - a_0 \in y$ (`ha₀y`); and the supersingularity hypothesis `hss`, that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the image of $j_\ell$ lies in `ssJSet q Ω`.
--
--   The remaining hypotheses, all retained as stated, are grouped as follows. The hypotheses `hover` and `hOrbit` are the comparison of $y$ with the place $s$: `hover` says that for every modular function $g$ of level $M'$ over $\mathbb Q$ lying in the integers of $R_0$, whose divisor is bounded by that of $j$ in the stated sense and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every $c \in k_0 \cap A$ whose residue in $A$ equals $s.\mathrm{evalAt}$ of that residue, the element $g - c$ of $C$ lies in $y$; `hOrbit` says that any maximal ideal $y'$ of $C$ containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the same compatibility with $s$ is carried to $y$ by some level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $C$, i.e. $b \in y \iff \tau b \in y'$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hS1` (thirteen conjuncts, summarised here) is the rigid chart axiom for the pair $(B_t, W_t)$: $C \subseteq B_t$; $B_t$ has fraction field $K_\ell$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $\mathcal O$; the fibre $B_t/\pi_0$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t$ meets $k_0$ exactly in $\mathcal O$, has maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, is a discrete valuation ring, induces $y$ on $C$, and is the localisation of $B_t$ away from its maximal ideal; for every $\mathbb F_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $\kappa$ of $\mathcal O$ there is a surjection $\rho : B_t \to \mathrm{DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing}\,q\,\kappa$ with kernel the elements of the maximal ideal of $W_t$, compatible with $\mathcal O \to \kappa$, and equivariant for the level automorphisms: each level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ acts on the coordinate ring through an element $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c)$ of [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of the character $\mathrm{det} \cdot (\,\cdot\,)^{q+1}$), with $c \ne 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$, and with the scalar $c$ also realised by a level automorphism whose matrix part is trivial; $B_t$ is stable under all level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$, $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ is the image of the maximal ideal of $W_t$ under some level automorphism; and an element of $B_t$ killed modulo the maximal ideal of $W_t$ by all level automorphisms from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hR7` (twelve conjuncts, summarised here) describes the inertia generator $\tau_0$ and the set `ends` of branch rings: $\tau_0^n$ is the identity on $B_t$ and no smaller power is, every level automorphism from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with a power $\tau_0^k$, $k<n$; `ends` has exactly $q+1$ elements, is permuted by the level automorphisms from $\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$, transitively so, is fixed elementwise by $\tau_0$, and distinct members are separated by an element that is a non-unit in one and a unit in the other; there is a finite-type $\mathcal O$-subalgebra $B_c$ of $K_\ell$, stable under the level automorphisms from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, contained in every member of `ends` and with every member of `ends` its localisation; a level automorphism mapping some member of `ends` into $W_t$ preserves $W_t$; and every $O \in$ `ends` is a Noetherian local subring of $W_t$ containing $C$ and meeting $k_0$ in $\mathcal O$, with no element of $O$ congruent to a constant modulo units in the stated sense, whose adic completion is identified by a ring isomorphism $\iota$ with the crossing model $W[[U,V]]/(UV - \pi_0^m)$ over the adic completion of $\mathcal O$: there are $c_x, c_y \in O$ with $c_x c_y = \pi_0^m u$, $u$ a unit, matching $U$ and $V$ up to units under $\iota$, with $c_y$ in the maximal ideal of $W_t$ and $c_x$ not, the element $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,q - a_0$ corresponding to a unit times $V^e$ with $e \ge 1$, the automorphism $\tau_0$ inducing an automorphism $\theta_0$ of the crossing model with $\theta_0(U) \equiv \zeta_c U$ and $\theta_0(V) \equiv \zeta_c^{-1} V$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal; moreover for each such $O$ there is a discrete valuation subring $W_x$ of $K_\ell$ dominating $O$ and $C$, with uniformiser $\pi_0$, meeting $k_0$ in $\mathcal O$, non-degenerate on a parameter $t$ and on $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,q$ in the stated polynomial sense, with $c_x$ in and $c_y$ outside its maximal ideal, inducing exactly $y$ on $C$ in one direction and not in the other, distinguishing $O$ from the other members of `ends`; and there is a finite-type $\mathcal O$-subalgebra $B_x$ of $B_t \cap O$, stable under the level automorphisms from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, with $O$ its localisation, whose members have non-negative order at the places where $j_\ell$ does, and containing an element witnessing that $O$ is not contained in certain valuation subrings. The last conjunct of `hR7` states that every valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ meeting $k_0$ in $\mathcal O$, containing $C$ and inducing $y$ on $C$, either contains $B_t$ or dominates some member of `ends`.
--
--   The hypotheses `hR9` and `hR10` are the two non-degeneracy clauses: `hR9` provides an element of $B_t$ outside every valuation subring of $K_\ell$ that meets $k_0$ in $\mathcal O$, has $\pi_0$ in its maximal ideal and keeps $j_\ell$ integral; `hR10` provides, for each maximal ideal $y''$ of $C$ containing $\pi_0$ which is not in the level-automorphism orbit of $y$, an element of $B_t$ outside every valuation subring of $K_\ell$ through which $y''$ is realised after transport by a level automorphism. The hypothesis `hn` identifies $n$ with $\mathrm{placeWidthChar}\,q\,M'$ at $s$, namely $\mathrm{jWidthChar}\,q$ of $s.\mathrm{evalAt}(j^{\mathrm{geom}})$ divided by the ramification index $\mathrm{placeRamificationJ}$ of $s$.
--
--   Finally the descent data: $G$ is the subgroup of $\mathrm{Aut}_{k_0}(K_\ell)$ generated by all level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ (`hG`); $K_0$ is the fixed field of $G$ (`hK₀`); $B_0$ is the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$ consisting of the $G$-invariant elements of $B_t$ (`hB₀`); and $W_0'$ is the valuation subring of $K_0$ induced by $W_t$ (`hW₀'`).
--
--   The conclusion is a single assertion: $B_0$ is formally smooth as an algebra over $\mathcal O = A \cap k_0$.
--
--   This is the formal smoothness clause for the quotient by the level group $G$ of the rigid supersingular affine chart of the modular curve of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$, in the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ diamond frame at the prime $q = 2$: the invariant subalgebra $B_0 = B_t^G$ of the chart is shown to be formally smooth over the ring of constants. It feeds the construction of the supersingular discrete valuation ring on the descended affine chart, where the special fibre of the integral model at a supersingular point is analysed.
